From: Win7 port restore
Subject: [PATCH] desktop: in-app changelog modal with a pre-baked bilingual corpus

The About panel's changelog button used to open the upstream GitHub
releases page in the browser — useless offline, and it showed upstream's
Installation instructions for builds this Win7 package does not ship.

Replace it with an in-app modal built from the project's own components
(Modal / SelectField / MarkdownRenderer — theming, dark mode included):

- changelogContentData.ts (generated): one entry per upstream release
  (all 40, v0.1.0 - v0.5.4) holding the exact Markdown the modal shows
  per language. The zh/en split of each releaseNotes body and the
  Installation/download-section removal are baked in at generation
  time, so rendering is a pure lookup.
- changelogContent.ts: language mapping with fallback — the Simplified
  Chinese locale gets the Chinese section, every other locale (zh-TW
  included) gets the English one, and a missing bucket falls back to
  the other language (37 of the 40 upstream bodies are monolingual).
  Bare issue refs (#1190) are linkified to the upstream tracker,
  skipping code spans and existing links.
- AboutSettings.tsx: the button opens the modal when the build's tag
  has a body (otherwise falls back to the browser); an in-modal
  SelectField switches between all bundled versions with a
  "current version <build>" indicator when browsing history; the Modal
  key remounts the dialog per switch so the body scrolls back to top.
  The now-unused GITHUB_RELEASES const and its releases-page jump go
  away with the button's old behavior.
- i18n: new settings.about.currentVersion key in all five locales.

Verified on the Win7 VM via CDP across zh/en/zh-TW/jp/kr: 43/43
assertions (content, issue-link counts, indicator localization,
fallback chain, 40-option switcher), zero console errors.

